course: dictionary
lesson: 4564
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer wants. In «a stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge, a hedge is a place a badger can hide, so it must be a row of bushes or small trees.
explain: «To hedge means to avoid giving a clear answer.» If someone is asked a question and will not say a plain yes or no, they are hedging. Ask yourself what is happening: is it a plant, or is someone dodging a question?
explain: «To hedge your bets» is a phrase whose meaning is not in each single word. It means protecting yourself by choosing more than one option, so if one plan fails, another is still there.
fact: A hedge is a row of bushes or small trees growing close together, forming a living fence.
fact: Hedges line gardens, fields and country lanes, especially in Britain and Ireland.
fact: Hedges give homes to birds, hedgehogs and insects, so they are habitats.
fact: To hedge means to avoid giving a clear answer.
fact: To hedge your bets means to protect yourself by choosing more than one option.
exercise: «Badger peered out of a hedge.» What is a hedge here? | A road with a steep bank | *A row of bushes or small trees growing close together | A bridge over a stream | A type of bird that sings at night
exercise: Dad asks, «Will you come to the party?» Ella says, «I might, I'll see.» What is she doing? | *Hedging, avoiding a clear answer | Hedging her bets with two gardens | Trimming a hedge in the garden | Hiding behind a hedge
exercise: Hedges give homes to which animals? | Fish and crabs | Camels and cacti | Cars and buses | *Birds, hedgehogs and insects
test: Nadia says «hedge your bets» means to bet money on a horse. What does it really mean? | Trim the hedge twice in one week | Bet money on a horse race | Hide from the rain under a hedge | *Protect yourself by choosing more than one option
test: Which sentence uses hedge as a verb? | «The hedge was trimmed in spring.» | *«She hedged when asked if she liked the gift.» | «Blackberries grew on the hedge.» | «The hedgehog ran under the hedge.»
exam: Jayden says a hedge is always a plant. Is he right? | No, hedges are always made of stone | Yes, a hedge is always a plant and never anything else | *No, hedge can also be a verb, meaning to avoid a clear answer | Yes, but only in Scotland
